# Applications

Applications add HTTP structure around `.rocci` templates. Standalone and
custom are two depths of this layer, not two products.

Stop here if you only preview components. You do not need Rocdown to ship
an app.

:figure[caption: "rocci run serves one HTTP origin. Datastar morphs HTML. Ship a server or an ad-hoc app."] {{
    :img[src: "/assets/diagrams/runtime.svg", alt: "rocci run compiles .rocci modules, writes generated or authored main.roc, and roc compiles a loopback HTTP origin. The preview window loads that origin. GET view returns a document, POST fragment morphs HTML by id, POST command returns empty SSE or 204, and GET live polls a stream. rocci build --release writes a server plus assets; rocci bundle writes an ad-hoc macOS app.", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"]
}}

| Depth | What you author | What `rocci run` does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Standalone | `@context`, `@init`, `@get:view`, optional fragment / command / live routes | Generate dispatch, `/assets`, `/health`, and authored live paths |
| Custom | `main.roc` plus `.rocci` modules | Compile sibling modules and start your Roc program |

Stay standalone until generated dispatch is in the way: extra routes, a
different platform, or a runtime the dispatcher does not express. Reuse
components; do not re-implement `Html`. How a request moves is the
[runtime matrix](/docs/reference/runtime/).

